<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Exploring My Community – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 67%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring My Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Market!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Introduce themselves or family members using basic personal information. Interactive: Take part in role-plays that mimic familiar everyday interactions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can introduce themselves and family members while sharing personal information about community places (e.g., "I'm Ana, and this is the park where I play." or "My dad works at the library."). Can take part in role-plays that mimic familiar everyday interactions about community places (e.g., "Where is the park?" "It's next to the library."). Can participate in role plays about common activities with family members (e.g., Mom says, "Let's take a walk." The child says, "Yes, let's go!").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 60%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Community Places Song" (e.g., to the tune of "The Wheels on the Bus") using flashcards for "park," "market," "school," and "library." Students perform simple TPR actions for each place (e.g., run in the park, shop at the market). Teacher asks, "What do you see?" while pointing to cards to activate prior knowledge of vocabulary. Focus on /p/ in 'park' and /m/ in 'market' pronunciation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows two large flashcards (e.g., 'market' and 'library'). Teacher models: "This is the market. It's big." and "This is the library. It's next to the market." Teacher then introduces a puppet, "Hello, my name is Puppet. Where is the park?" and models pointing to the park flashcard and saying, "It's the park. I play there." Teacher explicitly models polite expressions like "Excuse me, where is the library?" and "Thank you."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the market flashcard) "Is this the school or the market?" (Students chorally say: "The market!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the park flashcard) "Do we play here?" (Students nod and say: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing 'church' flashcard) "What sound is 'ch'?" (Students imitate the /ʈʃ/ sound).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring My Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful illustrated dialogue strip showing two children, Ana and Leo, walking through a community and stopping at different places like the park, market, and library. Simple speech bubbles with target language (e.g., "Where is the park, Ana?" "It's near the market, Leo. It's a big park!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and identifying short, simple questions about familiar places and activities in the community, and basic descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Visual):</b> Teacher reads the dialogue aloud with expressive voices and gestures, pointing to the pictures. After each short exchange, the teacher asks, "Who is talking?" (Ana and Leo) and "What place are they talking about?" (Students point to the correct picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher holds up individual flashcards of places (e.g., 'market', 'library', 'park') and asks, "Where do Ana and Leo talk about the market?" Students point to the corresponding panel on the dialogue strip.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (with action):</b> Teacher makes simple statements related to the dialogue and asks students to stand up for "True" and sit down for "False" (e.g., "The park is small." - Students sit down).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice asking "Where is it?" and answering "It's the [place].", "It's [preposition] the [place].", and using adjectives like "big," "small," "busy" with community nouns (park, market, school, library, church, store). Students also practice introducing family members using "This is my [family member]. My [family member] [verb] at the [place]."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Echo the Community":</b> Teacher holds up flashcards of community places and family members. Teacher points to a picture (e.g., 'park') and asks, "Where is the park?" Students chorally repeat, "Where is the park?" Then teacher points to another card (e.g., 'library next to park') and models, "It's next to the library." Students echo. Teacher adds, "The park is big." Students echo. Teacher then points to 'mom' and 'market' and says, "This is my mom. My mom goes to the market." Students repeat. Focus on clear pronunciation of /p/, /m/, /ʈʃ/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – "Find Your Neighbor":</b> Students work in small groups with a set of mixed flashcards (community places, family members, adjectives). The teacher calls out a place, e.g., "Market!" Students find the market card. Teacher then asks, "Where is the market?" One student points to the market and another student says a simple descriptive sentence, "It's busy!" or "It's next to the school!" The teacher guides using sentence frames on the board (e.g., "Where is the ___?" "It's ___ the ___.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Community Map Mystery":</b> Students work in pairs, each with a simple, illustrated "My Community Map" (showing places like park, market, school, library, church, store) and small picture cards of family members. Student A points to a place on their map without showing Student B and asks, "Where is the [place]?" Student B looks at their own map and answers using prepositions and adjectives, e.g., "It's in front of the school." or "It's a small market." Then, Student A introduces a family member related to a place: "This is my dad. My dad works at the office." and Student B points to the office on their map. Students switch roles. Encourage polite expressions: "Excuse me..." and "Thank you."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring My Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs: "A Walk in My Community."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will perform a short role-play mimicking familiar everyday interactions about community places, introducing family members, and asking/answering questions about location.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each pair receives a larger, simplified "Community Walk" map (showing park, market, school, library, church, store, playground, bus, road) and small cut-outs of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose two roles: "Navigator" (asking for directions) and "Community Helper" (giving directions and introducing family).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example Scenario:</b> Navigator wants to find the market.
                        <br>Navigator: "Excuse me, where is the market?"
                        <br>Community Helper: "It's next to the library. It's a busy market!"
                        <br>Navigator: "Thank you! Oh, and this is my mom. My mom goes to the market."
                        <br>Community Helper: "Nice to meet your mom! Look, there's a big park over there."
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students practice their short role-play, moving their fingers on the map to show movement. The teacher circulates, offering support and encouragement, focusing on natural interaction and polite expressions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A few pairs can volunteer to perform their role-play for the class.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "A Walk in My Community" role-play in pairs for the teacher or a small group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /p/, /m/, and /ʈʃ/ in target words (e.g., park, market, church).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3 community place nouns (e.g., park, market, library).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 1 adjective to describe a place (e.g., big, busy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses WH-question "Where is...?" correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Answers using Present Simple "It's..." and prepositions (e.g., next to, near).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Introduces a family member using "This is my [family member]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses polite expressions (e.g., "Excuse me," "Thank you").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates and interacts with partner.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "smiley face" card if they feel they can do the action, or a "neutral face" if they need more practice.
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can ask "Where is the park?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say "It's next to the market."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say "This is my mom."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Oral/Show of hands):</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to say today about our community? (e.g., "Park," "It's big.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was a little hard to say? (e.g., "Excuse me," or "between.")</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the speaking activities worked well? (e.g., pair work with maps, TPR engagement).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with forming full sentences or using prepositions? (e.g., more sentence frames, specific preposition practice with realia).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did students manage the pronunciation of /p/, /m/, and /ʈʃ/? (e.g., good progress, need more emphasis in future lessons).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will use these same vocabulary words and basic sentence structures ("It's the market," "The park is big," "My mom goes to the market") to begin writing simple sentences about community places.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>